WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:24.900
Hello, and welcome. If you're trying Tenali for the first time, I'm glad
you're here. Running a business means keeping track of so many little
things, doesn't it? We'll take this slowly, together. I'll show you where
your products, money and online shop connect. You can pause whenever you
need a moment.

00:25.000 --> 00:33.900
Choose Tenali AI in your workspace menu. Let's start with a little help.

00:34.000 --> 00:59.900
Let's start with a little help. Here in Tenali AI, you can simply say what
you're trying to do. Something like, help me prepare a purchase bill. You
don't need special business language. Start a new chat, type your question
here, and we'll work from there. This is available when AI is enabled for
your business.

01:00.000 --> 01:23.900
Suppose a supplier has sent you a bill. You can attach it and ask AI to
help prepare the supplier details, products and purchase together. Think of
this as a first draft, not a finished decision. Please take a moment to
review the amounts and anything missing. Only confirm the changes you're
comfortable saving.

01:24.000 --> 01:50.900
Have a longer catalogue? You don't have to start with an empty spreadsheet.
Choose Prepare product bulk workbook, and attach your catalogue. The reply
gives you a ZIP to download. Inside are the Excel sheet and any extracted
photos. Have a look before uploading, especially at the purple AI-written
values, prices and pictures.

01:51.000 --> 01:59.900
Choose Operations, then Accounts. Here we'll look after your cash and bank
records.

02:00.000 --> 02:25.900
Next, let's give your money a clear home. In Accounts, a name like Shop
cash or Business bank makes later payments much easier to follow. The
opening balance is just the money already there when you begin using
Tenali. Enter it once. That way, your starting money doesn't accidentally
get counted as a new receipt.

02:26.000 --> 02:51.900
This is Account Tally. It helps you see what you started with, what came
in, and what went out. For cash, compare this with your drawer. For your
bank, compare it with the statement. If the numbers differ, the transaction
list can help you trace why. Moving money between your own accounts isn't
another sale.

02:52.000 --> 03:01.900
Choose Operations, then Borrowing and Lending. We'll keep these money
movements clear together.

03:02.000 --> 03:26.900
Sometimes the business needs to borrow money. Here, choose Borrowed from
party and select the person or business it came from. Add the date and
amount. If you'd like Tenali to record the money arriving in your account
too, check that linked-account option. This is money to return, rather than
sales income.

03:27.000 --> 03:49.900
If you're the one lending money, choose Lent to party instead. The contact
tells you who should return it, and the account tells you where it left
from. It's worth checking that direction before saving. A loan and a
supplier payment can look similar in a bank statement, but they belong in
different places here.

03:50.000 --> 04:13.900
When it's time to settle, you only record what's moving today. Repaid to
party is for money you're returning. Collected from party is for money
coming back to you. The remaining balance helps you see what's still
outstanding. For a customer's unpaid sales bill, we'll use Receivables and
Collections instead.

04:14.000 --> 04:23.900
Choose Operations, then Purchases. Let's walk through a supplier's bill,
one step at a time.

04:24.000 --> 04:49.900
Let's look at buying goods for your business. In Purchases, choose the
supplier and the store receiving them. Copy the bill date and supplier's
bill number, then add the products, quantities and buying prices. If you've
paid some of it already, enter that payment too. Before posting, give the
tax and total a careful check.

04:50.000 --> 05:13.900
Here's a small detail that protects your stock figures. Has everything on
the bill actually arrived? If it has, choose Goods received now. If not,
it's perfectly fine to record the bill first and confirm receipt later. We
don't want stock appearing on your shelves before the goods are really with
you.

05:14.000 --> 05:23.900
Choose Admin, then Stores and Warehouses. Here's where each business
location gets its own place.

05:24.000 --> 05:46.900
If you have more than one shop or a warehouse, you can give each location
its own name here. Then choose the right location when buying, selling or
moving stock. You'll know what belongs where. When goods travel between
your stores, use a stock transfer, so it doesn't look like an extra sale or
purchase.

05:47.000 --> 06:12.900
Your online shop also needs to know where its goods come from. Link it to
the shop or warehouse that will fulfil orders. It uses that location's
stock, rather than creating another copy. You can allow collection from a
shop too. Before accepting payments, we'll also need the settlement account
and checkout setup in place.

06:13.000 --> 06:20.900
Choose Admin to find your business setup tools. We'll take a quick look
together.

06:21.000 --> 06:45.900
There are quite a few tools, so let's make the menus feel familiar. Admin
is mainly where the owner or administrator sets things up: business
details, stores, team access and the website. You don't need to do all of
this today. Start with what your business needs, and give each team member
only the access they need.

06:46.000 --> 06:54.900
Choose Operations for your everyday work. Your bills, stock and money tools
are gathered here.

06:55.000 --> 07:19.900
Operations is the menu you'll probably return to most often. It brings
together your contacts, money accounts, purchases, sales and products.
There's also a daily checklist in the Operations Portal. Think of it as a
helpful reminder at opening and closing time, rather than a replacement for
recording the actual work.

07:20.000 --> 07:28.900
Choose Dashboard in your workspace menu. Let's get a gentle overview of the
business.

07:29.000 --> 07:54.900
When you want a quick check, come to Dashboard. First choose the dates and
store you're interested in. Then you can look at sales, payments due,
pending purchases and low stock. If something needs attention, open its
work area for the details. These totals describe the selected records, not
necessarily the money in your bank.

07:55.000 --> 08:02.900
On the home page, choose Set up my business. We'll begin with your account.

08:03.000 --> 08:28.900
If you haven't set up your business yet, this is where you begin. Choose
Set up my business and follow the sign-in steps. Add your business name and
the workspace address you'd like to use. Already have an account? Just sign
in to that one. Keeping one workspace avoids splitting the same business
records across two places.

08:29.000 --> 08:37.900
After signing in, choose Admin, then Company Setup. Let's check the details
for your business.

08:38.000 --> 09:03.900
Let's make sure your business details look right on documents. Add the
name, address and contact information here. The GST switch should match
your business's actual registration. If you're registered, add the GST
number and check it carefully. Changing this switch changes how documents
are prepared; it doesn't register the business for GST.

09:04.000 --> 09:12.900
Choose Operations, then Product Inventory. Here's where your products and
stock are kept.

09:13.000 --> 09:37.900
Now let's add something you sell. We'll use a steel water bottle for our
example. Give it a name you'll recognise, and a short stock code, or SKU,
if that's useful. We're calling this one BOTTLE zero one. Before adding
another entry, it's worth checking whether the item is already in your
list.

09:38.000 --> 10:01.900
A brand tells you who makes it, and a category helps customers find it. You
can choose a saved name or type a new one. When an HSN is needed, choosing
a saved code brings in its description and tax rate. Please still check
that it fits this product. A convenient suggestion isn't a tax decision.

10:02.000 --> 10:27.900
Let's give our sample bottle a selling price of two hundred and fifty
rupees. That's the price the customer sees. The amount you pay the supplier
is separate, in its vendor offer or purchase. Keeping those two prices
apart makes your records easier to understand. If you use a discount rule,
check the resulting customer price too.

10:28.000 --> 10:53.900
A clear picture makes shopping easier. Upload a photo, or paste its public
link, and look at the square card preview before saving. Preview and crop
lets you adjust the frame without stretching the bottle. One thousand
pixels on each side is a good target. Choose a background colour, and keep
the whole product comfortably in view.

10:54.000 --> 11:19.900
We've named the bottle, but Tenali doesn't yet know how many you physically
have. In Owned Stock, choose your store and record twenty-four bottles as
opening stock. Do this once. If a received purchase already added those
bottles, don't enter them again here. We want the screen to reflect what's
actually on your shelves.

11:20.000 --> 11:29.900
Choose Operations, then Bulk Upload. This is where your prepared
spreadsheet comes into the business.

11:30.000 --> 11:57.900
For a longer list, Bulk Upload can save you time. Choose Products and
download the latest template. The Data sheet is where your entries go;
README explains the fields. Sizes and colours can be child rows below their
parent, linked by its SKU. Keep supplier offer details separate from
selling prices, and leave anything unknown blank.

11:58.000 --> 12:25.900
You can include pictures in that upload too. For a web picture, use its
image URL. For a local photo, put the filename in the sheet, then ZIP the
workbook with an images folder. Brands and categories have image fields as
well. A missing brand or category picture uses its first available product
photo, so the shelf isn't left empty.

12:26.000 --> 12:50.900
Once you upload, watch the successful, failed and remaining counts here. A
failed row doesn't undo the good ones. Download the results and read the
reason beside each failed row. Correct just those rows, and try them again.
There's no need to repeat the successful rows; they've already been saved.

12:51.000 --> 12:59.900
Choose Operations, then Sales and Quotations. Let's prepare a customer's
bill with care.

13:00.000 --> 13:25.900
Let's make a sale together. Start by choosing the customer. If they're new,
you can add them here. Check the date and store, along with the billing
details. For a sale where payment will come later, a proper customer record
is especially helpful. It gives that unpaid amount a clear person and bill
to belong to.

13:26.000 --> 13:51.900
Our customer is buying two bottles. Choose the bottle, enter two for
quantity, and check the price of two hundred and fifty rupees each. That
makes five hundred rupees in this example, with GST off. Take a quick look
at the whole bill before posting. These are sample numbers; use your own
agreed prices and applicable tax.

13:52.000 --> 14:15.900
Let's say the customer gives you three hundred rupees today. Enter three
hundred as received, choose Cash, and select Shop cash as the account. The
remaining two hundred is still due. You don't need to pretend the bill is
fully paid. Recording the real amount now makes the balance much easier to
follow later.

14:16.000 --> 14:41.900
Keep Auto-create receivable selected so the unpaid part stays on your list.
After a final check, post the invoice once. When the customer pays the
remaining two hundred rupees, record the collection against this bill. If
you notice a mistake, use the supported correction or cancellation flow,
rather than creating another copy of the sale.

14:42.000 --> 14:51.900
Now return to Operations, then Product Inventory. We'll check what changed
after that sale.

14:52.000 --> 15:16.900
Let's check what that sale changed. We started with twenty-four bottles,
and sold two, so this store should now show twenty-two. Stock History tells
you which movement changed the number. If it looks different, follow that
history first. Adding another opening balance would hide the problem rather
than explain it.

15:17.000 --> 15:26.900
Choose Operations, then Product Inventory. Let's decide which products
customers can see online.

15:27.000 --> 15:52.900
Ready to put the bottle on your website? Keep it active and choose Visible
in customer catalog. Check the picture, price and any size or colour
options. Save it, then look at the shop as a customer would. For an online
purchase, the product also needs a valid price and available stock at the
fulfilment location.

15:53.000 --> 16:02.900
Choose Admin, then Company Setup. Your online shop settings are here, with
a link to its designer.

16:03.000 --> 16:27.900
Your shop should feel like yours. In Storefront Design, open the theme or
homepage section you want to work on. You can change the words, pictures
and colours, and crop images to the recommended size. Selected items come
first; the others follow unless you choose selected only. Try the phone
preview before saving.

16:28.000 --> 16:36.900
Choose Storefront in the header menu. Then open a product to see what your
customers see.

16:37.000 --> 17:00.900
Once you're happy with it, share your shop link with customers. They can
browse on a phone, swipe through the shelves and choose the options they
need. The website uses the same product and stock records as your daily
work. That's the useful part: you aren't maintaining a completely separate
list just for online sales.

17:01.000 --> 17:10.900
Back in your workspace, choose Admin, then Payments and Delivery Setup.
We'll look at the options together.

17:11.000 --> 17:37.900
Some customers would rather talk before ordering, especially for larger
quantities. They can collect several products into a WhatsApp enquiry list
and send them together. You can then discuss prices and availability with
them. Just remember, it's a conversation at this stage. The enquiry itself
isn't a payment or a stock reservation.

17:38.000 --> 18:03.900
When you're ready for online payments, you can connect your own Razorpay
account in Payments and Delivery Setup. Begin in Test Mode and complete the
displayed checks before going live. There are provider approvals and
webhook settings to finish too. Please keep account keys private. This
guide doesn't connect an account or charge any money.

18:04.000 --> 18:29.900
For delivery, choose what works for your business. You can use your own
team or courier, or set up Shiprocket with its API user and pickup
location. Check the areas you serve and the delivery charges. In Online
Orders, you'll manage dispatch or collection. A shipment booking is only
the start; delivery still needs to be tracked.

18:30.000 --> 18:38.900
Choose Admin, then Storefront Domains. Here's where you connect your own
website address.

18:39.000 --> 19:06.000
If you'd like your own website address, add a domain you own in Storefront
Domains and follow the DNS instructions shown there. Take care to keep your
email settings intact. Verification and HTTPS can take a little time. And
that's our walkthrough. Thank you for spending this time with me. You can
come back to any chapter whenever it helps.
